Ongoing supportive supervision is needed because it 

* builds and maintains providers’ confidence and competence in delivering the intervention, ensuring they adhere to the model and handle challenging clinical cases safely;  
* offers technical and ethical oversight (e.g., managing risk, confidentiality limits);  
* provides quality‑assurance feedback and opportunities for skills development, which improve service delivery, job satisfaction and staff retention; and  
* protects providers’ well‑being by giving emotional support, helping them recognise and manage their own stress and referring them to external care when needed.  

Without regular supervision, providers—especially non‑specialists with little mental‑health experience—may struggle with distressing content, risk errors, and experience burnout.
